---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title="Speaking | Ravikumar Buragapu"
			description="Conference talks, interviews, and speaking topics covering production AI, agentic systems, platform engineering, developer productivity, and operational resilience."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<header>
				<h1>Speaking</h1>
				<p>
					I speak about the engineering challenges behind emerging
					technologies—from building productive engineering platforms
					to operating reliable, secure AI systems at scale.
				</p>
				<p>
					My focus is on the decisions that turn promising ideas into
					production systems: architecture, evaluation, security,
					operational resilience, and measurable engineering outcomes.
					I try to help audiences see not only what is possible, but
					what it takes to make it work reliably at scale.
				</p>
				<p>
					<a href="#talks">Past talks</a> · <a href="#invite">Invite me to speak</a>
				</p>
			</header>

			<section aria-labelledby="offered">
				<h2 id="offered">Talks I offer</h2>

				<article class="topic">
					<h3>The Hard Part of Agents Isn't the Model</h3>
					<p>
						Why production agent systems depend on identity,
						permissions, evaluation, observability, cost controls,
						and operational accountability—not just model quality.
					</p>
					<p class="label">Key takeaways</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Why model capability is only one part of agent reliability</li>
						<li>How to design permission boundaries for agents</li>
						<li>The role of evaluation in deciding how much autonomy an agent gets</li>
						<li>Operational patterns for trustworthy AI systems</li>
					</ul>
					<p class="audience">Audience: AI engineers, platform architects, engineering leaders, enterprise technology teams.</p>
				</article>

				<article class="topic">
					<h3>Earning Agentic Autonomy</h3>
					<p>
						How evaluation evidence, error budgets, policy
						enforcement, and runtime controls can determine
						which actions an AI agent is allowed to take.
					</p>
					<p class="label">Key takeaways</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Treating autonomy as an engineering decision, not a model capability</li>
						<li>Evaluating agent behavior under realistic conditions, including replay and attribution</li>
						<li>Setting risk-based autonomy tiers that move with measured reliability</li>
						<li>Connecting evaluation results to execution-time controls</li>
					</ul>
					<p class="audience">Audience: AI architects, researchers, engineering leaders, AI governance practitioners.</p>
				</article>

				<article class="topic">
					<h3>Engineering AI-Native Organizations</h3>
					<p>
						How internal developer platforms, AI-assisted
						workflows, and guardrails change software delivery,
						and what that means for engineering organizations.
					</p>
					<p class="label">Key takeaways</p>
					<ul>
						<li>How AI changes software development workflows</li>
						<li>The evolution of internal developer platforms</li>
						<li>Measuring developer productivity in AI-assisted environments</li>
						<li>Building organizations that can adopt AI responsibly</li>
					</ul>
					<p class="audience">Audience: CTOs, VPs and directors of engineering, platform engineering leaders.</p>
				</article>

				<h3>More topics</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Engineering Reliable AI Systems.</strong>{' '}
						Monitoring, failure recovery, human oversight, and measurable
						service objectives for AI in production.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Developer Productivity: From Metrics to Outcomes.</strong>{' '}
						Moving beyond activity-based metrics to developer experience
						and software delivery performance.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Operational Resilience for Data-Intensive Systems.</strong>{' '}
						High availability, observability, capacity, and recovery across
						complex enterprise platforms.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p class="note">Depth and focus can be adapted for technical, leadership, or mixed audiences.</p>
			</section>

